WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:05.760
2020 was in many ways a
rough year for Hollywood,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.360
with the COVID-19 pandemic

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.530
pushing back some of the
year's most anticipated movies.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.160
But the industry rallied
by releasing no shortage

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.530
of ambitious and exciting new projects,

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.180
which brought their own
set of breakthroughs

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.180
in the world of special effects.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.580
From massive battle and fight sequences

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.480
to some of the most
realistic practical effects

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.040
we've ever seen,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.470
here's a behind-the-scenes glimpse

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.632
at the biggest films of the year.

00:00:29.632 --> 00:00:32.220
Three, two, one!

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.340
To play the legendary warrior Mulan

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.120
in Disney's live-action film,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.470
Liu Yifei spent three months training

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.350
for up to seven hours a day.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.150
This demanding regimen paid off.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:44.360
The film's cinematographer, Mandy Walker,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.830
told insider that Yifei
was able to execute

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.440
about 90% of her own stunt work.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.580
In preparation for the film's
acrobatic fight sequences,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.470
Yifei and her castmates were schooled

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.990
in everything from weapons
handling and archery

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.690
to military marching and horseback riding.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.490
The performers did their
most dramatic midair stunts

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.480
while attached to wires or beams

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.935
so that it looked like their characters

00:01:05.935 --> 00:01:08.180
were flying or spinning through the air.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.313
All in all, the film's action sequences

00:01:10.313 --> 00:01:12.020
required 90 horses,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.290
80 trick riders, 4,000 prop weapons,

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.440
281 sets of armor for the Chinese army,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.311
and 730 pieces of armor
for the Rouran army.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.760
How do you create a terrifying
monster out of nothing?

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.520
It turns out, with a lot of
clever camera and stunt work.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.500
This scene from "The Invisible Man,"

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.138
where the titular villain throws

00:01:36.138 --> 00:01:38.950
Elisabeth Moss' character
across the table,

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.880
is actually a bunch of
shots stitched together.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.780
First, the crew filmed Elisabeth

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.540
as she was about to be thrown.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.740
Next, they swapped in
the actor's stunt double

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.337
and filmed her being thrown

00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:51.940
by another stunt
performer in a green suit.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.280
Then they swapped in Elisabeth again.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.190
In postproduction, VFX
artists combined the plates,

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.610
then digitally erased
the green-suit performer

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.690
and the ropes used for the stunt.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.070
For it all to match up perfectly,

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.720
the crew had to shoot the whole thing

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.450
with a motion-controlled camera,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.610
which could be programmed
to perform precise,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.480
repeatable camera movements.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.930
That wasn't the only bit of
camera trickery in the film.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.380
On the last day of shooting,
when filming this scene,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.203
the camera team had to
get what they called

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.686
"an impossible shot" --

00:02:18.686 --> 00:02:21.590
one that involved passing a
camera out of a moving car

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.215
into another moving car,

00:02:23.215 --> 00:02:25.565
all in one smooth handoff.

00:02:28.097 --> 00:02:31.700
"Extraction" marked the
directorial debut of Sam Hargrave,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.410
the veteran stunt coordinator
behind "Atomic Blonde"

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.270
and a number of Marvel movies.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.120
Naturally, then, the Netflix thriller

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.380
was full of daredevil stunt work --

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.880
just as much from the camera crew

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.220
as from the actors and stunt performers.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.370
The centerpiece of the film

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.820
was what the crew referred
to as "the oner" --

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.810
an extended fight scene made to look like

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.550
one continuous shot.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.728
Sam is familiar with big,
long action sequences

00:02:54.728 --> 00:02:56.632
from working on the famous one-shot fight

00:02:56.632 --> 00:02:57.830
in "Atomic Blonde."

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.310
But this one for "Extraction"

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.610
required Sam to take us

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.580
right into the middle of the action,

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.750
even strapping himself to
the hood of a chase vehicle.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.700
Just to get this one smooth shot

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.430
taking us from outside to inside a car,

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.650
he had to unstrap himself
from that one car,

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.370
run over to the other vehicle,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.740
and then repeat the same shot twice.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.550
This is what you call a stitch point,

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:19.770
one of the invisible cuts

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.170
that joined about 36 different segments

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:26.328
into what looks like one
long, seamless 12-minute take.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.080
For the third installment in
the "Bill &amp; Ted" franchise,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.100
special effects legend Kevin Yagher

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.180
created special bodysuits for scenes

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.000
where Bill and Ted meet
future versions of themselves.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.010
The process began with
lifecasts of Alex Winter

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.560
and Keanu Reeves' bodies,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.810
which were then used to sculpt

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:45.550
the shape of the actors' suits,

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.000
both the muscle suits they
wear in the prison scene

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.900
and Keanu's fat suit worn
in a different scene.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.383
Kevin and his team sculpted

00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:54.383
and spray-painted tiny details

00:03:54.383 --> 00:03:57.040
like pores and veins onto the muscle suits

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.860
and added bags of beads
into Keanu's fat suit

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.444
to get just the right jiggle in his belly.

00:04:02.444 --> 00:04:03.730
[beads shaking]

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.731
The rollout of the "Sonic" movie

00:04:07.731 --> 00:04:09.020
got off to a rough start

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.160
when the first trailer was
met with backlash from fans

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.820
who objected to this Sonic's

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.280
disturbingly realistic appearance.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.363
In response, the VFX artists

00:04:18.363 --> 00:04:20.304
gave the character an overhaul,

00:04:20.304 --> 00:04:23.100
making his eyes bigger,
his mouth more animated,

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.360
and his nose and limbs more cartoonish,

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.870
more like the original
design in the video games.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.960
Just about everyone liked
the redesign better,

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.948
including the man behind the hedgehog,

00:04:31.948 --> 00:04:33.960
actor and comedian Ben Schwartz.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.850
Ben not only lent his voice to Sonic,

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.010
but also provided facial motion capture

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.220
to inform the character's animation.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.538
On set, they used different
stand-ins for Sonic

00:04:42.538 --> 00:04:44.360
to give costars like James Marsden

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.360
something tangible to interact with,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.260
whether it was a blue stuffy
or just a ball on a tripod.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.290
We saw CGI turn man into hedgehog.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.657
What about turning man into dog?

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.130
In this year's remake of
"The Call of the Wild,"

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:02.700
veteran motion-capture
performer Terry Notary

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.220
provided an incredible mo-cap performance

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.100
for the movie's canine protagonist, Buck.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.877
Having Terry on set provided
a raw-size reference

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:13.095
for the other actors and gave costars

00:05:13.095 --> 00:05:15.320
someone to interact with emotionally.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.450
For Buck's design, the filmmakers used

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.790
the reference of a real-life shelter dog,

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:21.850
whose own name happens to be Buckley.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.100
To help guide the animation

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.600
of the many dog and wolf
characters in the film,

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.520
the team brought in five other dogs

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.570
for a mo-cap playtime session.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:30.403
But don't worry;

00:05:30.403 --> 00:05:32.830
no pups were involved in
the filming of this scene,

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.180
where Buck and John Thornton
navigate a treacherous river.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.370
The filmmakers enlisted a stuntperson

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.050
to shoot the real deal on a
river in British Columbia.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:43.020
They then combined the on-location shots

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.100
with blue-screen footage of Harrison Ford

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.670
paddling on a canoe gimbal,

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.570
filmed on a motion-controlled camera

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.445
that allowed for some particularly

00:05:50.445 --> 00:05:52.261
cinematic, sweeping shots.

00:05:52.261 --> 00:05:54.590
Finally, VFX artists digitally added

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:56.710
Harrison's canine friend into the mix

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:58.238
in postproduction.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.690
The character in "Onward"
that presented Pixar

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:05.270
with the most challenges was this guy,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.900
known simply as Dad.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.750
He's introduced when Ian,
voiced by Tom Holland,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.740
carries out a spell to
conjure up his late father,

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:15.230
but only half of his body appears.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:18.410
Pixar's animators spent a lot
of time on Dad's first scene

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.270
to make sure it would capture the force

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:22.200
and unpredictability of magic.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.270
They even took turns blasting each other

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:25.873
with a leaf blower to get a feel

00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:28.150
for what the conjuring
effect should look like.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.010
From there, the challenge was giving life

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.917
to a pair of pants, a
task that forced Pixar

00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:35.452
to bring its cloth
technology to a new level.

00:06:35.452 --> 00:06:37.790
The studio used a
process called simulation

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:39.570
to program how a character's clothing

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.580
should respond to his or her movements

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.660
and the different forces acting on them.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:47.210
But because Dad's upper half
is all clothes and no body,

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.770
simulation played an even
bigger role than usual.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.410
To figure out how a person would move

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.180
if he were missing his top half,

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.860
Pixar's animators took turns

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:57.180
putting on green suits with khakis

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.460
and moving around in
front of a green screen.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.750
Later, they digitally
removed their top halves

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.390
and used the footage as reference

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.007
for what Dad's body
language might look like.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.450
"The Call of the Wild"
wasn't the only film

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:12.600
where CG animals figured prominently.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.885
The majority of scenes
in "Dolittle" feature

00:07:14.885 --> 00:07:18.790
live-action humans and CG
animals interacting in some way.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:19.860
The tricky part of that:

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.370
making sure the humans were looking

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.330
in the right places during filming.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.880
Nailing the eyelines was
especially challenging

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:27.870
with a character like Betsy the giraffe.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.240
To help guide the actors
on set, the crew used

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.250
stuffy props as stand-ins
for the CG animals

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:34.790
and got creative from there.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.290
Like with Betsy, for instance.

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:39.210
They ended up sticking a
giraffe head on a long pole,

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.450
which got driven around on a motor cart.

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.530
In another scene, Robert Downey Jr.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:47.050
needed to ride the ostrich
Plimpton into Buckingham Palace.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.110
So they devised a gimballed riding rig

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.630
that they could later
replace with a CG creature,

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.741
voiced by Kumail Nanjiani.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.810
The action-heavy "Birds of Prey"

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.370
involved a lot of physical preparation,

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:02.265
with each actor's training program

00:08:02.265 --> 00:08:04.473
tailored to her particular character.

00:08:04.473 --> 00:08:07.980
Since Harley Quinn is a whimsical
and unpredictable fighter,

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.330
Margot Robbie's stunt work
involved a lot of gymnastics.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.465
Black Canary's fighting
style was supposed to be

00:08:13.465 --> 00:08:15.350
more grounded with a lot of legwork,

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:17.660
so Jurnee Smollett-Bell's preparation

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.350
combined tae kwon do and kickboxing.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.800
And Mary Elizabeth Winstead adopted judo,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.370
jujitsu, and karate to play Huntress,

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.745
the deadly-accurate assassin
who's all about precision.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.750
Early on in Netflix's "Project Power,"

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.283
there's an unforgettable scene

00:08:35.283 --> 00:08:37.208
where Machine Gun Kelly's character

00:08:37.208 --> 00:08:39.720
spontaneously bursts into flames.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:43.070
To figure out how to turn
MGK into a human fireball,

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.797
the effects team first
set stuntpeople on fire

00:08:45.797 --> 00:08:47.860
in a series of camera tests.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.820
Then they came up with a breakthrough:

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.897
an LED suit that would be embedded

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:54.410
within layers of burn prosthetics.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:57.030
The LEDs were programmed
to illuminate the actor

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.880
as his character is seen
exploding in flames.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.022
The gruesome prosthetics
helped hide the LED strips,

00:09:03.022 --> 00:09:05.290
along with all the
wires and battery packs.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.210
For MGK, this meant spending five hours

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.210
in the makeup chair every day of shooting

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.127
to get hundreds of prosthetic pieces

00:09:12.127 --> 00:09:15.120
glued on over 95% of his body.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.813
The process was so intensive
that the rapper started

00:09:17.813 --> 00:09:19.935
just sleeping in his body prosthetics,

00:09:19.935 --> 00:09:22.102
and did so for five days straight,

00:09:22.102 --> 00:09:23.980
even wearing them for
a night out on the town

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.989
with costar Jamie Foxx.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:30.070
For Netflix's "Jingle Jangle,"

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:33.170
the biggest challenge was
creating a fantastical world

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:36.000
where magic and
spontaneous musical numbers

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.460
would feel right at home.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:39.620
To make Jeronicus fly in this scene,

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.500
the crew attached Forest
Whitaker to wire rigs,

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:44.350
later removed in postproduction.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.280
Another particularly fun sequence,

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:47.771
the kids' factory escape,

00:09:47.771 --> 00:09:49.446
was shot in front of a blue screen,

00:09:49.446 --> 00:09:52.042
with the two young actors
rigged to the wooden chest.

00:09:52.042 --> 00:09:54.420
VFX artists later added in the tunnel

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.899
and the giant fan and fireball.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:00.888
In this comic-book adaptation,

00:10:00.888 --> 00:10:03.146
Charlize Theron's character
is supposed to have

00:10:03.146 --> 00:10:05.993
6,000 years of martial arts experience,

00:10:05.993 --> 00:10:09.640
ranging from Greco-Roman
wrestling to Filipino stick work.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.090
Charlize had to do intense combat training

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.905
so she could look effortless handling

00:10:13.905 --> 00:10:16.030
both modern and ancient weapons.

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:17.700
Of course, the star is no stranger

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.250
to this kind of hardcore training,

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:20.870
having done a lot of her own stunts

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:23.080
for "Atomic Blonde" and "Mad Max."

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.730
The "Old Guard" fight coordinator,

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.480
Danny Hernandez, told
Insider that Charlize

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.660
and Kiki Layne spent four months training

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:31.600
for the film's major fight sequence,

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:33.378
where their characters engage in epic

00:10:33.378 --> 00:10:36.170
hand-to-hand combat
inside a plane mid-flight.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.900
The payoff? The actors were able

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.460
to pull off 90% of the fight themselves,

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:42.108
without stunt doubles.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.550
How does this shot become this?

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:48.570
Through the process of CG lighting,

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.086
which was especially important

00:10:50.086 --> 00:10:51.552
to building the magical worlds

00:10:51.552 --> 00:10:54.410
of Netflix's animated
film "Over the Moon."

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:57.030
Creative lighting helped
emphasize the dreamy light

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:57.970
coming from the moon,

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.260
which plays a central role in the story.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:02.298
In this shot, the lighting
team is perfecting

00:11:02.298 --> 00:11:05.360
the big, bright moon
reflections in Fei Fei's eyes.

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:06.868
And in this shot, they're balancing

00:11:06.868 --> 00:11:08.510
the different light sources coming from

00:11:08.510 --> 00:11:11.260
the moon, the kitchen,
and the colorful lanterns.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.140
These decisions about the
intensity and placement of lights

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:16.260
are laid out early on in color scripts,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.010
which are like artistic blueprints,

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.440
expressing the mood of any given scene.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.340
For the scenes set on Lunaria,

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.720
the artists wanted the
light to be overpowering

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.530
and the colors vibrant and bold

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:28.900
to give an otherworldly
aura to the moon kingdom

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.517
and invoke a sense of awe in the audience.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:36.127
The horror movie slash political satire

00:11:36.127 --> 00:11:38.010
"The Hunt" follows a group of liberals

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.990
hunting conservatives for sport,

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:41.792
so there were lots of opportunities

00:11:41.792 --> 00:11:43.830
for practical gore behind the scenes.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.703
But the most talked-about sequence

00:11:45.703 --> 00:11:47.360
was the climactic kitchen fight

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.730
between Betty Gilpin and Hilary Swank.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:52.180
To make the head-to-head
between these two characters

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:54.060
feel as messy as possible,

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.780
the crew designed a special set

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.600
that was made to be destroyed.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.390
The set included foam floors,

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:01.023
a chimney rigged with squibs,

00:12:01.023 --> 00:12:03.456
and a bunch of household-item
breakaway props

00:12:03.456 --> 00:12:04.860
that were built to be broken,

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:06.376
just like this glass window,

00:12:06.376 --> 00:12:07.770
which shatters in this scene.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:09.690
It was like a twisted jungle gym,

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:11.973
meant to serve as the ideal backdrop

00:12:11.973 --> 00:12:13.980
for all the chaotic violence.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:15.960
So, did we miss anything?

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.223
And what do you think were

00:12:17.223 --> 00:12:19.610
the most impressive effects of 2020?

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.376
Let us know in the comments.

